{Color gamut is defined as the range of colors that a particular device can produce or record.}
{It is usually shown by an enclosed area of the primary colors of the device on the chromaticity diagram.}
{The most commonly known color gamut might be “NTSC” (established in 1953 by the US FCC), however, it is not a standard color gamut.}
{“sRGB” is the standard color gamut established by the IEC in 1999.}
{The reason for standardizing a color gamut was to promote an easier way for color reproduction.}

{Sentence 1: "The color gamut of a monitor is defined as the range of colors that it can produce or record."}
{Sentence 2: "The color gamut of a device, such as a monitor or printer, is usually shown by an enclosed area of primary colors on a chromaticity diagram."}
{Sentence 3: "Color gamut is a three-dimensional concept, consisting of hue, saturation, and brightness, and is often represented as a two-dimensional graph for ease of visualization."}
{Sentence 4: "Standardizing a color gamut, such as sRGB, helps ensure consistent color reproduction across different devices."}
{Sentence 5: "Different color gamuts have different applications, such as AdobeRGB for graphic design and DCI-P3 for digital cinema."}

